WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.807
This is your first short practice exercise.

00:00:02.807 --> 00:00:08.857
Before you run the code, read each question and predict which source should appear at the top.

00:00:08.857 --> 00:00:13.967
Try a scholarship appeal question, a VPN troubleshooting question, and a graduation question.

00:00:13.967 --> 00:00:20.292
Then run the retriever and compare the results with your prediction.

00:00:20.292 --> 00:00:24.292
Do not worry if a second or third result is not perfect.

00:00:24.292 --> 00:00:29.493
The main goal is to understand how the ranking changes when the meaning of the query changes.

00:00:29.943 --> 00:00:37.250
Also resist the temptation to change the embedding model immediately if one result surprises you.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:41.115
Read the source text and the returned chunk first.

00:00:41.115 --> 00:00:48.295
Sometimes the model is doing something reasonable that was not obvious from the title.

00:00:48.295 --> 00:00:56.028
This habit—predict, retrieve, inspect, explain—is useful when you later evaluate much larger RAG systems.
